---
/**
 * 25 · Rosette, Five
 *
 * Five circles, which is the one count with no mirror pair top to bottom,
 * so the two halves of the mark are genuinely different shapes and the
 * silhouette is a pentafoil rather than anything symmetrical twice over.
 *
 * Five used to be the most constrained count on this grid, and the reason
 * was a rule rather than the geometry: while the face had to fit inside a
 * cleared circle in the middle, an odd-row silhouette could almost never
 * hold an even-row circle with equal air, and the only five that survived
 * had its petals pulled in so close that they stopped reading. Once the
 * face was allowed to sit on the flower itself that constraint went with
 * it, and the five opens up: this one carries its petals at a ring
 * distance of nearly four tenths of their diameter, which is the ratio
 * the reference uses, and the lobes come back.
 *
 * The ring is rolled off the vertical, which is what keeps the shoulder
 * stepping four pixels at a time like the canonical circle instead of
 * jumping six.
 *
 * Calmest of the four. Pale petals, a saturated pentagon where all five
 * agree, and the face across it in the palest tone. The hue is pushed off
 * the sea-teal of the six and toward jade, because the two are the only
 * pale-petalled marks here and at 24 pixels a shared hue would make them
 * one mark seen twice.
 */
import MarkFrame from '../MarkFrame.astro';
import { facePathsAt } from '../../../data/mark';

interface Props { size?: number }
const { size = 128 } = Astro.props;
---

